#clinical-finding(title: [The Clinician's Delicate Balancing Act])[

The same Norwegian qualitative study documents the clinician side of a field the paper has
elsewhere described from the patient side. The 16 healthcare professionals — all purposively
recruited because they already use psychologically and behaviourally informed approaches —
operationalised the biopsychosocial "map" in two overlapping orientations: one framing
fatigue as cumulative stress and overload with impaired regulation, the other as
maladaptive learning, symptom sensitisation, and self-sustaining predictive processes
("retraining the brain") @Bakken2026RecoveryConceivable.

Critically, these clinicians did not report confident certainty. They described supporting
recovery within a "delicate balancing act": being sufficiently challenging to support change
while avoiding loss of trust, and navigating between validation and reappraisal, hope and
realism, and agency and blame. They reported residual diagnostic uncertainty and explicit
concern about how their approach would be viewed "if future research were to establish a
treatable biological dysfunction with little or no role for the processes they currently
targeted." Some reported being criticised for "not believing patients" or minimising
suffering, and several advised caution regarding certain patient organisations
@Bakken2026RecoveryConceivable.

This is the clinician-side mirror of the epistemic-injustice tension the paper documents
from the patient side (@sec:psychogenic-debate): the contestation over CFS does not only
harm patients — it also leaves clinicians insecure, defensive, and sometimes at odds with
patient communities. It does not excuse psychosomatic attribution, but it shows that the
BPS camp is internally heterogeneous and uncertain rather than a monolith confidently
asserting the illness is psychological.

*Certainty:* 0.35 (qualitative, reflexive thematic analysis; purposive sample of HCPs who
endorse the approach, so it cannot represent the range of clinician views).

*Evidence type:* qualitative HCP perspectives (Bakken 2026); contextualised against the
paper's existing patient-side critique @Bayliss2014BarriersPrimaryCare @Schomerus2026PsycheRole.

*Severity:* N/A — the study reports clinician experience, not patient outcomes.

*Limitations:* Single Norwegian specialist-healthcare context; the HCP sample is selected
for a specific therapeutic orientation, and the patients they describe were likely
receptive to it. The study does not address patients who do not improve or who deteriorate
under these approaches, which the authors themselves flag as a needed direction.

*Replication:* Not yet replicated; this is one qualitative study. It converges with
patient-side recovery-narrative work @Ingman2025RecoveryThematic in its emphasis on shifts
in illness understanding, but the HCP perspective is novel.

*Consequence:* Humanises the contested field without endorsing it — showing that even
clinicians inside the psychobehavioural camp are uncertain and vulnerable to the same
polarisation the paper describes, which bears on how patients and clinicians might rebuild
trust rather than entrench opposition.

] <cf:hcp-delicate-balancing-act>
